[Music]  cloud shell editor makes it easy for you  to run code from your web browser and  deploy it to the cloud  and google kubernetes engine makes it  easy to deploy  manage and scale your containerized  applications using google infrastructure  so you might be wondering can i write  the code for my google kubernetes engine  apps in cloud shell editor  yes you definitely can with its cloud  code integration  cloud shell editor gives you the power  to write code for your kubernetes  application  test and debug the app locally create  google kubernetes engine clusters  and finally deploy your app to a cluster  in this video  we're going to learn how to use cloud  shell editor to deploy an application  to google kubernetes engine let's go  first visit ide.cloud.google.com to  launch the cloud shell editor  on the status bar at the bottom of your  screen click cloud code  from the cloud code menu click new  application and then select kubernetes  application for the type of sample app  we want to create  after that we'll get a list of google  kubernetes engine samples to choose from  let's go with the go hello world option  next  select a folder in your cloud shell  environment to save your application to  and give it a name  if that looks good click create new  application  collateral editor loads the files for  your sample app into a new workspace  you can see the list of files in the  explorer panel located on the left side  of the screen  now let's go ahead and test our app  locally for this next section you will  run your application on a local  kubernetes cluster in cloud shell  in the top menu click terminal and then  new terminal  this opens up a new cloud shell terminal  window at the bottom of your screen  in your terminal run minicube start to  start your local mini cube cluster  once your cluster is set up you will see  a confirmation message  from the cloud code menu select run on  kubernetes and confirm that you want to  use the minicube context  the output panel displays the progress  as your app is built and deployed to the  local minikube cluster  once your app is built you can launch it  by hovering over the url link displayed  in the output panel and clicking  open web preview  congratulations if you see this page  that means you successfully run your  first app on kubernetes using cloud code  now let's go ahead and modify the app  to learn more about this hello world app  you can browse the files in the explorer  tab  at a high level it includes a go hello  world web app in maine.go  that returns a templated it's running  response to all receive requests  there's also a load balancer service  called go hello world external  and hello.service.gov that exposes the  app by describing a kubernetes service  now let's go ahead and modify the app  select the main.go file and replace  it's running with its redeploy  the file saves and rebuilds  automatically you can monitor your app's  progress in the output panel  this is cloud code's watch mode but it's  something you can turn off if you want  once your app finishes building and  deploying refresh your open tab to see  the updated app  to analyze your app while it's running  use the log viewer to monitor its logs  open the command palette by selecting  view find command  type cloud code view logs and click on  it  this opens up the log viewer which  allows you to filter and navigate the  logs for your app  in the deployment section select go  hello world  now refresh your app in the browser  come back to the log viewer and click  the logs refresh button to see new logs  you should see a new message for the  latest get request so far you've been  running your app locally  now it's time to push your app to a  remote cluster first  you'll need to create a new google  kubernetes engine cluster to deploy your  app to  click on the cloud code kubernetes icon  in the navigation bar  this kubernetes explorer makes  kubernetes development easier by  simplifying cluster creation  and management hover over the kubernetes  explorer bar and click on the plus sign  select create gke cluster  in the create cluster wizard select your  google cloud project  you'll need to select some properties  for the cluster you're going to launch  such as the zone or region  cluster name and any other selections  [Music]  click create cluster  cluster creation may take a few minutes  but once it's created your cluster is  listed within the kubernetes  explorer view finally let's deploy your  app to the new gke cluster  from the cloud code menu select run on  kubernetes  confirm your newly created cluster as  the context for your deployment  confirm the default option for your  image registry  once your app is successfully deployed  you can launch it via the link displayed  in your output panel  congratulations you've just created and  run your first kubernetes app on gke  don't forget to delete your cluster  using the gke explorer to avoid any  unwanted charges  just right click your cluster and select  delete cluster  [Music]  want to try this out yourself check out  the interactive tutorial linked in the  description below  make sure to subscribe to the channel  and stay tuned for more cloud shell  editor videos  [Music]

